= Correlation — Technical =

== Pearson coefficient ==
For finite nonzero variances:

<pre>
ρ_XY = E[(X-μ_X)(Y-μ_Y)] / (σ_X σ_Y)
</pre>

The sample Pearson coefficient estimates this population quantity.

== Invariance ==
Pearson correlation is invariant to positive affine rescaling and changes sign under one negative scaling.

It specifically measures normalized linear dependence.

== Zero correlation ==
For jointly Gaussian variables, zero correlation implies independence.

Outside special families this implication fails.

Example symmetric nonlinear dependencies can have:

<pre>
ρ = 0
</pre>

while Y remains strongly determined by X.

== Alternatives ==
Other measures include:

* Spearman rank correlation;
* Kendall rank association;
* distance correlation;
* mutual information;
* copula-based dependence measures.

They answer different statistical questions.

== Causal interpretation ==
A causal effect concerns intervention or counterfactual structure, not association alone.

Conditioning or regression cannot generally identify causality without design or assumptions.

